# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Repository-wide ignores.
#  A pattern without a slash matches at any depth, so a single `.env` line
#  covers 01-kafka/.env, 02-monitoring/.env and every stack added later.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Real environment values — never committed. Each stack ships a .env.example.
.env
.env.local
*.env.bak

# Secrets and credentials
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
secrets/

# Local state some stacks bind-mount instead of using named volumes
data/
logs/

# macOS
.DS_Store
._*

# Editors and IDEs
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# Python (dbt, helper scripts)
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# dbt
dbt_packages/
.user.yml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  dbt build output, with one exception
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  target/ holds compiled SQL, run results and logs — all regenerated on every
#  invocation and none of it worth tracking.
#
#  manifest.json is different. It is not a build artifact here but a contract
#  between two stacks: Cosmos reads it to decide what Airflow schedules. A
#  manifest that has fallen behind the models means tasks that silently never
#  run — the DAG renders perfectly, one task short, and nothing says so.
#
#  Tracking it is what lets CI compare the committed manifest against the
#  committed models. Generating it in the workflow instead would compare a
#  file against the project it was just generated from, which always agrees
#  and proves nothing.
#
#  Note the pattern: `target/*` and not `target/`. Git does not look inside an
#  excluded directory, so a negation under a plain `target/` never matches —
#  the file stays ignored and the exception appears to do nothing.
#
#  The cost is a large JSON with noisy diffs, and a conflict whenever two
#  people change models at once. Regenerate rather than resolve:
#      cd 07-dbt && make manifest
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note the leading **. A pattern containing a slash anywhere but the end is
# anchored to the repository root, so `target/*` matched only a target/ beside
# this file and left 07-dbt/project/target/ entirely untracked-but-unignored.
# The plain `target/` it replaced worked precisely because a trailing slash is
# the one form that still matches at any depth.
**/target/*
!**/target/manifest.json